TYPES OF SPROCKETS
A sprocket is a toothed wheel that meshes with a roller chain
to transmit rotary motion and torque. Unlike a gear, it does
not mesh with another sprocket; it drives or is driven by chain.

KEY VARIABLES / FUNDAMENTALS
P   = chain pitch (distance between pin centers)
N   = number of teeth
d   = pitch diameter  ≈  P / sin(180° / N)
D   = outside diameter
B   = bore diameter
W   = tooth / hub width
C   = center distance between sprockets
i   = speed ratio  = N_driven / N_driver
v   = chain speed  = (P × N × rpm) / 12   (ft/min, P in inches)

More teeth = smoother engagement, less wear, higher speed capability.
Fewer teeth = more compact, more chordal action (pulsation).
Match sprocket pitch exactly to chain pitch (ANSI / ISO / BS).
Lubrication, alignment, and correct wrap angle matter as much as type.

------------------------------------------------
1. SINGLE STRAND
   One row of teeth. Used with a single roller chain.
   Simple, light, general-purpose drives.

2. DUPLEX
   Two rows of teeth. Used with double-strand chain.
   Higher load capacity than single strand.

3. TRIPLEX
   Three rows of teeth. Used with triple-strand chain.
   Heavy loads and high power transmission.

4. MULTI-STRAND
   Four or more rows. Extreme-duty / high-capacity drives.

5. BUSH TYPE
   Finished bore plus interchangeable bush.
   Fits many shaft sizes. Easy install / replace.

6. SPLIT SPROCKET
   Two-piece design, bolted together.
   Install or remove without sliding the shaft out.

7. SPLINE BORE
   Internal splines match shaft splines.
   Precise fit, high torque, no keyway slip.

8. PLATE SPROCKET
   Flat plate, no hub (or minimal hub).
   Light, cheap, often welded or bolted to a hub later.

9. QD (QUICK DETACH)
   Uses a QD bushing.
   Fast mount, accurate axial position, easy change-out.

10. DOUBLE SINGLE
    Two single-strand sprockets joined on one hub.
    Independent chain alignment on each side.

11. DRIVE SPROCKET
    Power-input sprocket (usually smaller, higher speed).
    Transmits motor/gearbox torque into the chain.

12. DRUM SPROCKET
    Wide-face or drum-style.
    Common on conveyors to drive belt or chain over a large wrap.

13. TAPER-LOCK / TAPER BUSH
    Tapered bush + screws lock the sprocket to the shaft.
    Very versatile: many bores from one sprocket, strong grip,
    easy removal. Often the shop favorite for maintenance.
